WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:04.660
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:10.820
The faithful simulation of human skin is incredibly
important both in computer games, the movie

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:13.860
industry, and also in medical sciences.

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:19.490
The appearance of our face is strongly determined
by the underlying structure of our skin. Human

00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:25.710
skin changes significantly with age. It becomes
thinner and more dry, while the concentration

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:31.500
of chromophores, the main skin pigments diminishes
and becomes more irregular. Those pigment

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:38.230
concentrations are determined by our age,
gender, skin type and even external factors

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:45.579
like, for example exposition to UV radiation
or our smoking habits. As we age, the outermost

00:00:45.579 --> 00:00:51.440
layer of our skin, the epidermis thins, the
melanine, haemoglobin and water concentration

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:53.149
levels drop over time.

00:00:53.149 --> 00:00:55.120
As you could image having a plausible

00:00:55.120 --> 00:01:00.180
simulation considering all the involved actors
is fraught with difficulties.

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:06.450
Scientists at the University of Zaragoza came
up with a really cool, fully-fledged biophysically-based

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:12.630
model that opens up the possibility of simply
specifying intuitive parameters like age,

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:18.990
gender, skin type, and get, after some processing,
a much lighter skin representation ready to

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:22.720
generate photorealistic rendered results in
real time.

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:28.680
Luckily, one can record diffusion profiles,
also called scattering profiles that tell

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:34.360
us the color of light that is reflected by
our skin. In this image, above, you can see

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:40.000
a rendered image and the diffusion profiles
of a 30 and an 80 year old person. The idea

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:46.110
is the following: you specify intuitive inputs
like age and skin type, then run a detailed

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:51.420
simulation once, that creates these diffusion
profiles that you can use forever in your

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:56.560
rendering program. And all this is done in
a way that is biophysically impeccable.

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:02.649
I was sure that there was some potential in
this topic, but when I first saw these results,

00:02:02.649 --> 00:02:06.520
they completely crushed my expectations. Excellent piece of work!

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:10.200
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

